      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How long does forced property sale take in UAE?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A court-ordered forced sale of jointly owned UAE property takes approximately 6 to 18 months from the date of filing the partition and sale application. The steps are: court accepts the case and appoints a valuer (1-3 months), valuation report produced (2-4 weeks), court sets reserve price and orders listing (1-2 months), property marketed and offers received (varies significantly by market), sale completed with court oversight, and proceeds distributed (1-2 months). Timeline is longer if the property is mortgaged, as lender consent is required for the sale to proceed.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What happens to property equity in UAE divorce?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Under UAE\'s separate property regime (Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 for Muslims, Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 for non-Muslims), equity belongs to the registered title holder(s). For jointly registered property, equity is split according to proven ownership shares (typically the shares listed on the title deed). If the property is in one name but both parties contributed to the purchase price or mortgage, the non-titled spouse can file a contribution claim under Article 51 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 and Article 318 of the Civil Code to recover their proportionate equity share.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Can I buy out my spouse\'s share of our UAE property?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Yes. A buyout is the most common resolution for jointly owned property in UAE divorce. The buying spouse pays the other party the agreed value of their ownership share, and the title is transferred to the buyer at the DLD. The price is typically based on a market valuation from a DLD-accredited valuer. If there is a mortgage, the buying spouse must either refinance into their sole name (requiring bank credit approval) or retain the joint mortgage with a formal repayment agreement -- though banks prefer full refinancing. The transfer itself is done at a DLD Registration Trustee centre.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Do I need both spouses to sell an off-plan property?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'If the off-plan property is registered with the DLD (interim registration for unfinished units), both registered owners must consent to any transfer or novation. The developer\'s consent is also required under the Sale and Purchase Agreement (SPA) terms -- most UAE developers charge a transfer fee (typically 1-2% of the purchase price) for novating an SPA to a new owner. If only one spouse is registered as the buyer in the SPA and with the DLD, they can novate or sell independently unless a court precautionary attachment has been registered against the unit.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What DLD fees apply when transferring property at divorce?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
